  "freshnessHelpTitle": "How we estimate freshness",
  "freshnessHelp": "The roast date shows calendar days. Freshness uses an estimated age: frozen time is subtracted and each day with the bag open counts as about 1.7 days. A coffee roasted 12 days ago and opened 11 days ago can therefore show an estimated age of 20 days.\n\nCoffee releases CO₂ after roasting. Darker roasts usually release it sooner than lighter ones; espresso usually needs more rest than filter (Smrke et al., 2018). We use a model of that release to estimate the phases, not a measurement of your beans.\n\nOpening a bag speeds up aroma loss (Smrke et al., 2022). If you enter the roaster’s recommended rest, we adjust the curve so that filter’s prime phase starts at that age.\n\n“Your sweet spot” is learned from your ratings of this coffee and similar ones. The phases are a guide: taste your coffee and follow the roaster’s advice.",
